#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152935} :activity/id #uuid "1a693dfa-5b4d-5813-b09f-df9a73d9e43a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/id #uuid "6de9a5a5-ab7e-5d26-821c-9f50ad7ae54d" :topic/math-academy-id 2787} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153434 :tutorial/content "A quantifier is a special symbol (operation) that may transform a predicate into a statement.\n\nThe **universal quantifier** \"$\\forall$\" means \"for all.\" The formula $$\\forall x \\in U, \\, P(x)$$ means that *every* $x$ in the universal set $U$ gives a true statement when substituted into the predicate $P.$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+,\\, \\sqrt{a} > 0$$\n\nreads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **For all** $a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+,$ $\\sqrt{a} > 0$\n\nThis can be further rephrased using plain English as simply:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The square root of any positive real number is positive.*\n\nSometimes, we can omit the \"$\\in U$\" part if the domain is clear from the context and write $\\forall x, \\, P(x).$\n\nFor example, if it's clear that $n$ is an integer, then the following is a valid statement involving the universal quantifier:\n\n$$\\forall n,\\, (2 \\mid n)$$\n\nIn plain English, we might say the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The number two divides any integer.*\n\nNote that this statement is *false*." :tutorial/id #uuid "6b69eeeb-3757-5954-bd3d-96c7a93010dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7991 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ba03659f-f524-5dbb-ae6c-372a02577563" :step/math-academy-id 145572 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152086 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151812 :question/id #uuid "8c1240c3-a5ac-5c44-8381-4b82c199f591" :question/math-academy-id "e-12741"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "875d7f2a-43ec-5635-8e72-48ae83d0339b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049008}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401879}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "c8132653-5a1b-5476-a04e-ce1b65c3f133" :step/math-academy-id 145573 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152937}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153447 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the formal definition of the universal quantifier in connection to predicates of one variable.\n\nThe **universal quantification** of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as $$\\forall x \\in U, \\, P(x).$$ This statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the *entire* universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate equals the universal set $U{:}$ $$T_P = U$$\n\nFor example, consider the quantified statement\n\n$$\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{R},\\, \\sqrt{a} > 0.$$\n\nNotice that the universal set is $U = \\mathbb{R}$ and the truth set of the underlying predicate\n\n$$P(a): \\:\\: \\sqrt{a} > 0$$\n\nis $T_P = (0,\\infty).$ Since $T_P$ doesn't coincide with the universal set, the statement $\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{R},\\, \\sqrt{a} > 0$ is false." :tutorial/id #uuid "13634493-c706-51c0-adcf-200f064583eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8309 :tutorial/title "The Formal Definition of the Universal Quantifier"} :step/id #uuid "7f06bd0e-607f-5cfc-b053-5817229eb606" :step/math-academy-id 145576 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152087 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151810 :question/id #uuid "61af13e3-ff6a-5d2e-b888-9ec31afe00a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "114dedd9-ed3d-5d25-8616-030ea4bd84c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049552}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407844}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "e2e9fee2-4bef-5bcf-9273-f2b635d59f5c" :step/math-academy-id 145577 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153446 :tutorial/content "The existential quantifier \"$\\exists$\" means \"there exists\". The formula\n\n$$\\exists x \\in U, \\, P(x)$$ means that there is *at least one* element in the universal set $U$ that gives a true statement when substituted into the predicate $P.$\n\nFor example, $$\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, 2 \\not\\mid n$$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **There exists** $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ **such that** $2 \\not\\mid n.$\n\nThis can be further rephrased using plain English as simply:\n\n$\\qquad$ *There is an integer that is odd.*\n\nSimilar to the universal quantifier, we can simply write $\\exists x, \\, P(x)$ if $U$ is clear from the context." :tutorial/id #uuid "5a890270-27c1-52c0-a987-6948933acb97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8308 :tutorial/title "The Existential Quantifier"} :step/id #uuid "daf23f50-7d2e-5502-8fae-1f6557f34364" :step/math-academy-id 145581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151813 :question/id #uuid "60f06f02-4fa0-5725-8a87-64497a292a2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12742"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93249d2-e5f0-5767-98da-c33b837b41c0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407845}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "8535f2c2-e521-5cfb-8d8c-b5d82a4ebcc6" :step/math-academy-id 145582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153448 :tutorial/content "Finally, let's consider the formal definition of the existential quantifier.\n\nThe **existential quantification** of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as $$\\exists x \\in U, \\, P(x).$$ This statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for at least one element $x\\in U,$ i.e., if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate is not empty: $$T_P \\neq \\emptyset$$\n\nFor example, consider the quantified statement\n\n$$\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, 2 \\not\\mid n.$$\n\nNotice that the universal set is $U = \\mathbb{Z}$ and the truth set of the underlying predicate\n\n$$P(n): \\:\\: 2 \\not\\mid n$$\n\nis $T_P = \\{\\pm 1,\\pm 3, \\pm 5, \\ldots \\},$ the set of odd numbers. Since $T_P$ is not empty, the statement $\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, 2 \\not\\mid n$ is true." :tutorial/id #uuid "aa981bd2-681c-57a1-b6a0-c3b9110e0021" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8310 :tutorial/title "The Formal Definition of the Existential Quantifier"} :step/id #uuid "67c09639-5532-5323-9fdc-8266af5180c4" :step/math-academy-id 145585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151811 :question/id #uuid "48c68085-0e37-5a70-b927-5046bc62a756" :question/math-academy-id "e-12736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"} :step/id #uuid "a8d4d64a-55cb-58ec-9d64-db4c619082d1" :step/math-academy-id 145586}] :activity/title "Universal and Existential Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152377}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046679} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407850} :activity/id #uuid "b645e310-5887-5acb-8fa1-ced6f8dd1a3a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/id #uuid "6de9a5a5-ab7e-5d26-821c-9f50ad7ae54d" :topic/math-academy-id 2787} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45813537-dd63-5755-9b5d-ac39d95f30f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407820} {:answer/id #uuid "7a78fc40-89ca-5529-8635-b81c30e2e488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407822} {:answer/id #uuid "b45c7085-6af1-58af-82bc-fd751002e4cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407827} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8c0bae-8d50-5107-896c-3794d38f0b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} {:answer/id #uuid "e752ccd6-f76a-546c-833f-e724e076bf9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407836} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f97a28b-3f8b-5723-9948-a37f37c51db9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ab7419f-dd45-5016-a048-0ef13ef60278" :question/math-academy-id "q-183552" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(n):$ ${n}^{2}\\in N$ and $n+5>1$ \n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${n}^{2}\\in N$\" is the set $N,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+5>1$\" is $N.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=N\\cap N=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1ac83e9e-98ff-5466-a798-9dee1a326560" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03eab922-5a85-5026-a3d7-30ed995fcbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "There exists $y\\notin Q$ such that $y$ is prime." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407813} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4c46df-0280-55f0-ab7d-60c928de713c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "Every prime number $y$ is rational." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407814} {:answer/id #uuid "945c3424-1cff-57b2-9472-12cdecf1701c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "There exists $y\\in Q$ such that $y$ is prime." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407824} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d1dbbf-3579-5af2-80d8-490559725897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "For all $y\\in Q,$ $y$ is prime." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407830} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5dfd88-d11e-5abc-a681-95fc1039e544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "There exists $y\\in Q$ such that $y$ is not prime." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407824} :answer-field/id #uuid "afb31b48-45dd-5d1d-bcf6-b59e8b41fafe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c6bc667-fec4-55ad-9a19-ce10aa56b016" :question/math-academy-id "q-183517" :question/problem "$\\,$ $K(y):$ $y$ is prime\n\nGiven the predicate above, which of the following expresses the statement $∃y\\in Q,\\,K(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The existential quantifier \"$∃$\" means \"there exists.\" So, the statement \n$∃y\\in Q,\\,K(y)$ \nreads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $y\\in Q$ **such that** $y$ is prime.\n\n**Note:** This statement is true."} :step/id #uuid "33b8dae3-4134-5bde-8e80-7adcb9dba955" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327c8c8e-43d3-56fa-bb06-eb3e173dcf46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407816} {:answer/id #uuid "6d34e765-6636-5a3e-9d1c-5838b8df6736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407821} {:answer/id #uuid "84e1b5bc-354e-5ae0-8448-f44b3ca5b554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407823} {:answer/id #uuid "a51db10d-82d0-586d-9dc5-b7f7a52b5801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407825} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0ec875-5a2b-517c-a707-3581499b69c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407833} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d365a0d-0df0-5d17-b62a-1cd26b68c01b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "25efa028-94e4-52c8-9fb8-df2dc5b08098" :question/math-academy-id "q-183509" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(y):$ $y<y+1⇒{y}^{-1}\\in Q.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${Z}^{-},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is ${Z}^{-}.$\n- $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$y<y+1$\" is ${Z}^{-},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{-1}\\in Q$\" is ${Z}^{-}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{Z}^{-}}\\cup {Z}^{-}=∅\\cup {Z}^{-}={Z}^{-}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={Z}^{-}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3a898701-cef3-5e02-9a1d-25cb45a087cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d500fe4-57b9-52b5-83ea-9bda860eab27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407815} {:answer/id #uuid "339444a3-4357-5fdf-8924-442d097e39f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407817} {:answer/id #uuid "3588a127-8777-5e98-bb9b-b646349772e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407818} {:answer/id #uuid "424368b9-436c-5519-8a3e-d05dcde6b1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407819} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b87858-b44a-5014-877f-9dc575b84c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407835} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b33442d-c9c0-5d34-8c05-2ee2c223153a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "303b12dc-4682-5eea-846a-0e1ea50fc6ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-216465" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(m):$ $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is ${2k:k\\in Z}.$\n- $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer if and only if $m$ is even, we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ doesn't coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4b4b0b11-a323-5bae-b207-166228e36996" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a58e92df-9945-5743-8088-4250afd4e285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407826} {:answer/id #uuid "c523af15-9d38-59e1-a24b-c6e9af255c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} {:answer/id #uuid "c64e638f-c63c-542f-96c1-65992079bca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ba58d-5e8a-5213-afcf-34f6a5938516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407831} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf6d984-9d9e-5c92-a6cf-995e484dfb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407826} :answer-field/id #uuid "589eb3e5-2666-5827-8cea-4a656b6bf3b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5404de7a-e750-5076-ba3f-b3642f799e6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-216495" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(n):$ $n$ is odd or $n+1$ is odd\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$n$ is odd\" is the set of odd natural numbers, and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+1$ is odd\" is the set of even natural numbers.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={\\text{odd natural numbers}}\\cup {\\text{even natural numbers}}=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "bc39dc3f-3c61-5781-b3e2-cef58fc7b9f7"}] :activity/title "Universal and Existential Quantifiers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407812}]}
